re-safe handshake yields current Core authority identity/epoch, permitted Host identity/assurance, contract-set/version ranges, and compatibility status even when normal contracts do not overlap.

## PM-BOOTSTRAP-002 — CORE_INCOMPATIBLE

If bootstrap succeeds but normal contract overlap fails, Manager can truthfully enter `CORE_INCOMPATIBLE` rather than generic connection failure. Zero-overlap at bootstrap fails closed.

## PM-BIND-001 — Binding/Invalidation Matrix

Every authority-bearing object declares its reaction to Host identity/rebind, Security-State version, Core epoch, Auth/session epoch, client instance, Provider incarnation, execution incarnation, and contract/schema generation.

Allowed reactions include `INVALIDATE`, `RESET`, `REVALIDATE`, `RECONCILE`, `PRESERVE_HISTORICAL`.

## PM-OPMODE-001 — Operational/Degraded Modes

Per-capability/source operational state can distinguish full, read-only, stale-read-only, Auth degraded, Provider degraded, projection degraded, Core incompatible, Core unavailable, experimental-only, etc. Each declares allowed reads/writes and disclosure behavior. No degraded mode creates a Host bypass.

## PM-OPMODE-002 — Auth-Degraded Rule

If Auth authority is unavailable, new high-risk mutation or newly elevated disclosure fails closed unless an already-issued claim/grant explicitly supports bounded offline verification under its contract. Auth outage never creates a weaker fallback prompt.

## PM-OPMODE-003 — Core-Unavailable Scope

`CORE_UNAVAILABLE` describes loss/degradation after Manager already has a valid UI/session path; it is not a promise that Manager can cold-start when application admission/Core substrate itself is unavailable.

