# Z. CONTRACT REGISTRY, VERSIONING, AND CAPABILITY LIFECYCLE

## PM-CONTRACT-001 — Stable Contract Identity

Every load-bearing normative contract and every proof subject has a stable ID, owner, semantic version, status, and definition fingerprint. Section numbers are presentation only.

## PM-CONTRACT-002 — Semantic Change Requires Version Change

A stable ID cannot keep the same semantic version while its normative definition changes.

## PM-CONTRACT-003 — Monotonic Accepted-Contract Registry

Accepted contract remains recorded until explicitly preserved/moved/superseded/removed-with-rationale.

## PM-CONTRACT-004 — Immediate-Prior-Candidate Regression Diff

Regression Diff compares the current candidate against both the immediately prior roadmap candidate and the monotonic accepted-contract registry. Repository state is historical evidence, not automatically the review baseline.

## PM-CONTRACT-005 — Canonical Contract Package

Prime emits machine-readable schemas/fixtures for Rust/native/TypeScript consumers from one contract registry/package; implementations do not manually reinvent safety semantics.

## PM-WIRE-001 — Lossless Wire Semantics

Freeze lossless integer representation, native string/byte representation, canonical typed serialization, duplicate-key rejection, structural/cardinality/nesting bounds, canonicalization algorithm/version, schema/framing/version axes, and anti-downgrade behavior.

## PM-WIRE-002 — Unknown Safety-Bearing Fields

Unknown safety-bearing request fields fail closed. Response additions are ignorable only where contract explicitly marks them additive/safe.

## PM-WIRE-003 — Historical Decodability

Retained evidence/audit/transaction records bind old contract/schema versions. Historical definitions/decoders remain available at least for their retention horizon.

## PM-CAP-001 — Capability Lifecycle

`INTRODUCE → COEXIST → DEPRECATE → prove consumer migration → RETIRE`.

Manager does not silently reinterpret/retire capabilities still consumed by Shell/Terminal/other first-party clients.

